  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published ML framework extend assembly modeling to condensate phase behavior, bZIP logic circuits, and condition-dependent LLPS.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational modeling of how molecular interactions give rise to assembly and function under defined constraints. Polymer theory and MD of polyampholytic IDP sequence pairs indicate that interfacial and finite-size effects shape ternary versus binary LLPS, and the associated preprint links these effects to homeostatic noise buffering through phase multiplicity. A second preprint repurposes natural bZIP dimerization interactomes from three species as Boolean logic substrates, evaluated through mass-action modeling and genetic optimization. A published framework, LLPSense, combines protein language model embeddings with environmental parameters to predict condition-dependent phase separation. The items do not share a single system. What they share is explicit treatment of geometry, interaction networks, and environmental conditions in governing assembly behavior. The newer element is predictive and design-oriented use of sequence-derived and kinetic models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
